<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">USS Cole bombing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The USS Cole bombing was a suicide attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> on the American destroyer USS Cole while it was refuelling in the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, on 12 October 2000. Two men steered a small boat packed with explosives against the ship's hull, killing 17 American sailors and wounding dozens. It was one of the deadliest attacks on a US warship since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uss-cole-bombing.jpg" alt="USS Cole bombing" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DoD photo by Sgt. Don L. Maes, U.S. Marine Corps. (Released) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USS_Cole_(DDG-67)_Departs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> had tried a similar attack on the destroyer USS The Sullivans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 3 January 2000, part of the 2000 millennium attack plots, but the boat was so overladen with explosives that it sank. The October attack was discussed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> al-Qaeda summit of 5 to 8 January, attended by Khalid al-Mihdhar, later one of the September 11 hijackers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>'s prime minister Abdul Karim al-Iryani afterwards named al-Mihdhar as a key planner who had been in the country at the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Under Commander Kirk Lippold, Cole moored at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> at 9:30 for a routine fuel stop and began refuelling at 10:30. At about 11:18 local time a small fiberglass boat carrying more than 1,000 pounds of explosive approached her port side and blew up, tearing a gash 40 by 60 feet. Much of the blast entered a space below the galley, killing crew lining up for lunch, and thirty-seven were injured. The ship's rules of engagement had forbidden guards to fire on the boat without permission from the captain or another officer.</p><p>The crew had the flooding under control after three days. The British frigate HMS Marlborough was the first ship to help, and the wounded were taken to the Landstuhl Regional Medical Center in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the worst through a French military hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>. Cole was carried home aboard the heavy-lift ship MV Blue Marlin and rebuilt at Pascagoula, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Investigators of the FBI and NCIS worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in a hostile setting. A Yemeni judge sentenced Abd al-Rahim al-Nashiri and Jamal al-Badawi to death in 2004, but by May 2008 every defendant convicted in Yemen had escaped or been freed; Abu Ali al-Harithi, Fahd al-Quso and al-Badawi were later killed in American drone strikes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> courts held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> liable, and in 2015 a judge also found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> complicit.</p><p>Neither Bill Clinton nor George W. Bush answered the attack militarily before 11 September 2001; according to Condoleezza Rice, Bush did not want to respond to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">al-Qaeda</a> one attack at a time. The Navy reworked its force protection, and on 30 October 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> agreed to pay $335 million to the families of the Cole victims and of the 1998 embassy bombings, while denying responsibility.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “USS Cole bombing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
